## Decision: I'm going to use re-frame's pattern of using a map of maps.

```clojure
(def app-db
  {:users {:by-id {1 {:id 1 :name "John Doe"}
                   2 {:id 2 :name "Jane Doe"}}
           :all-ids #{1 2}}
   :posts {:by-id {1 {:id 1 :title "First post" :user-id 1}
                   2 {:id 2 :title "Second post" :user-id 2}}
           :all-ids #{1 2}}})
```

This structure is easy to navigate and update.

##Decision: We will add a `subject_viewer_config` property to the workflow configuration. This property will be an object with keys for each subject viewer that can be configured. Each key will be an object with properties that can be configured for that subject viewer.

For example, a workflow using the multi-frame subject viewer might have the following configuration:

```json
{
  "subject_viewer": "multiFrame",
  "subject_viewer_config": {
    "multiFrame": {
      "marksPerFrame": "perFrame",
      "framePositioning": "reset"
    }
  }
}
```

A workflow using the subject-group subject viewer might have the following configuration:

```json
{
  "subject_viewer": "subjectGroup",
  "subject_viewer_config": {
    "subjectGroup": {
      "subjectCellWidth": 200,
      "subjectCellHeight": 200,
      "subjectCellStyle": {
        "border": "1px solid black"
      },
      "subjectViewerGridColumns": 3,
      "subjectViewerGridRows": 3
    }
  }
}
```

The `subject_viewer_config` property will be optional. If it is not provided, the subject viewer will use its default configuration.

## Decision: We add an `alias` property to the `Op` section. It takes a list of strings, each string being a fully qualified op name.

### Example
```kotlin
Op("add") {
    alias = listOf("math.add", "math.plus")
    // ...
}
```
